<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinity</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trinitas, Trinitarianism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trinity is the Christian doctrine that one God exists in three divine persons, God the Father, God the Son (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>) and God the Holy Spirit, held to be coeternal and to share one essence. Early Christians and the fathers of the Church developed it from the late 2nd century onwards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> and the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 4th century established it as part of the creed. Most major Christian denominations today are Trinitarian. Those who reject the doctrine are called nontrinitarians, and their positions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unitarianism">Unitarianism</a>, binitarianism and modalism; many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinitarians">Trinitarians</a> regard them as heretical.</p></div>
